From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 13:47:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] The start order made again with the include search's asks
 (#2873)

file_cache_count_miss, which every compile with a failed include probe
runs, was inlined before the asks and is a function of its own with
them (850 bytes). Left out of the list it sat in the cold part of
.text, a page of code more for every process: with an earlier version
of the asks and main's list, musl's units, a process each, read 1.9%
more instruction TLB misses than main's on the 9700X, in every round of
two comparisons.

The same commands' traces of this tree's build name 1,441 functions
where the list had 1,440: that one more. Seven of the C library's names,
which read the processors online, move up among the small commands'
functions. Made once more from the build linked with this list, it has
the same functions, two lines in another place. With it, musl's units
read 0.96% and 1.03% fewer instruction TLB misses than main's.

Part of #2873.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TiDLnvTX4sXnEVEUdMWepR
Signed-off-by: David Gonzalez Martin <davidgmbb@gmail.com>
